[section, $md:
    # The MON format

    Moth Object Notation (MON) uses Moth's constructor syntax to store literal
    data. Named entries give each record field a value. A schema tells the
    reader what type each field expects. See @../language-overview/#mon-syntax
    (Constructor syntax and MON) for the forms the language and format share.

    A MON reader never evaluates Moth expressions, including expressions the
    compiler could fold. Rust applications can use the bounded decoder and
    deterministic writers for documents and nested values in the standalone
    `moth-mon` crate. Import it as `moth_mon`. Compiler clients may use the convenience re-export `moth::mon`
    with the same API and Rust types. Compiled Moth programs have no runtime
    codec support. Moth-native operations, `$mon`, automatic schema extraction
    and the static project builder remain deferred separately.
    The format below specifies square-bracket collections and maps plus schema
    defaults for choice payloads. Consult @../progress (Progress) for codec
    implementation status. In data, a contextual `::Variant` takes its choice
    from the schema. Moth source qualifies the variant with its choice name.

    The Rust API uses explicit exact numeric schemas without profile selection
    or aliases. Schemas select the width or scale directly, independently of
    Moth source inference and defaults.
]

[section, $md:
    ## Use MON from Rust

    Supply a schema rather than a compiler type or a file path. This example
    selects declared fixed widths, checks the typed result and handles a
    structured failure with the same prepared schema. Add a dependency on
    `moth-mon` for an independent application. Existing compiler clients can
    change the import to `moth::mon` without changing the API.

    [codeblock, $code "rust":
        use moth_mon::{
            Field, MonErrorCode, Schema, SchemaType, Value, decode_document,
        };

        fn main() -> Result<(), Box<dyn std::error::Error>> {
            let schema = Schema::record(vec![
                Field::required("count", SchemaType::I64),
                Field::required("ratio", SchemaType::F32),
                Field::required("half", SchemaType::F16),
            ])
            .prepare()?;

            let value = decode_document(
                "count = 3_000_000_000, ratio = 0.5, half = 0.1",
                &schema,
            )?;
            assert_eq!(
                value,
                Value::Record(vec![
                    ("count".into(), Value::I64(3_000_000_000)),
                    ("ratio".into(), Value::F32(0.5)),
                    ("half".into(), Value::F16(0.099_975_585_937_5)),
                ]),
            );

            let error = decode_document("__LoOp = 1", &schema)
                .expect_err("reserved labels fail");
            assert_eq!(error.code, MonErrorCode::InvalidIdentifier);
            println!("{error}");
            Ok(())
        }
    ]

    Successful values own their data and outlive the input. `MonError` keeps
    structured code, path, span and detail while supporting `Display` and
    `std::error::Error`. Preparing a schema once and decoding multiple
    documents around it keeps the same numeric widths and limits. The `moth-mon` crate documentation contains the full
    executable example with defaults, fixed-width values and encoding.
]

